MediaCodec VP8: send a payload descriptor the receiver can read, and pick a decoder

A VP8 call between two handsets showed a black remote tile while every counter said the
stream was healthy: 30 frames captured, 30 encoded, RTP arriving, the jitter buffer
filling, pictures assembled, the decoder called 30 times a second -- and 0 pictures out
of it, with no decoder error and no MediaCodec error callback. Measured on an SM-M146B
(Android 15) against a FreeSWITCH echo, 2026-09-25.

The decoder was blameless. encode_more_vpx declared the VP8 payload descriptor to be one
byte:

    unsigned payload_desc_size = 1;

while pjmedia_vpx_packetize writes FOUR for VP8 -- X=1, I=1 and M=1, so a 15-bit
PictureID follows (vpx_packetizer.c:91). The payload was therefore copied to `p + 1`,
over three of the four descriptor bytes that had just been written, and output->size
described the packet as one byte of descriptor rather than four.

What went out was a packet whose first byte still advertised the extended descriptor
while bytes 1..3 were video. The receiver believes that byte: pjmedia_vpx_unpacketize
reads X, then reads I, L, T and K out of what is now payload data, so it strips a length
it computed from video content. Measured at 4, 5 and 6 bytes on consecutive packets of a
single call. The decoder is then handed a frame that begins partway into the first
partition, which no VP8 decoder can read and none complains about -- MediaCodec consumed
every input buffer, returned no error and produced no picture.

vpx.c:664 has always had this right, which is why VP8 worked whenever libvpx was the
implementation in use and failed whenever this file was. That difference reads as "the
hardware decoder is broken", and it is not: c2.exynos.vp8.encoder produces correct
1088x612 VP8 with the start code 9d 01 2a in place, verified on the wire.

Proof, same handset and same call type, before and after the one-line change:

  descriptor on the wire   b0 + payload (3 of 4 bytes overwritten)  ->  b0 80 80 03
  desc_len at the receiver 4, 5, 6 -- varies with picture content   ->  4, every packet
  frame handed to decoder  starts 4 bytes into the payload          ->  byte-exact

This patch also:

  * discovers the DECODER through MediaCodecList and probes that it can be created,
    which patch 0002 already did for the encoder and left undone here. The static list
    it replaces holds OMX.* names that Android 15 no longer implements, and
    AMediaCodec_createCodecByName() returns a handle for them anyway -- so the codec
    registered with a verified Codec2 encoder and a decoder the platform does not have.

  * repairs the software-decoder fallback in and_media_enum_info, which walked the
    software list with the ENCODER's index and name and then tested a condition the
    hardware loop had already made true, so it could only ever `continue`. A handset
    whose hardware decoder list missed lost the whole codec instead of falling back.

  * counts the decoder's input and output callbacks, the indices taken and the buffers
    queued, and reports them rate-limited when the decoder starves. "failed to get input
    Buffer [0]" wrote itself 2825 times in one call and could not say whether MediaCodec
    had ever offered an index -- which is the difference between an initialisation fault
    and a consumption fault. These counters are what proved the input path healthy and
    moved the search to the wire.

  * checks the first keyframe of each VP8 stream for the start code and says so, once,
    when it is missing. This is the regression guard for the defect above: it is the only
    signal that distinguishes "the far end cannot decode us" from "the far end is quiet",
    and it costs one comparison per keyframe.

  * returns an input buffer the previous decode acquired and never queued. decode_vpx and
    decode_h264 assemble a picture across packets and queue only on the last, so every
    early exit -- an unpacketize error, an oversized picture, a failed queue -- used to
    drop the index. The pool is four buffers on an M14, so four such frames starve the
    decoder for the rest of the call.

  * zeroes width/height/stride in on_format_changed and believes each only when
    AMediaFormat_getInt32 says it has a value. The getter leaves its destination untouched
    for an absent key, and "stride" frequently is absent: this callback reported
    stride:-1275068304 for the encoder on an M14, which is stack garbage. On the decoder
    that number becomes dec_stride_len and write_yuv walks the output buffer in steps of
    it.

There is deliberately NO decoder bad-list. One was written and it condemned
c2.exynos.vp8.decoder three seconds into every call, for the defect above -- a component
blamed for bytes that reached it already corrupt. "Produced no picture" is not evidence
about a component: a decoder legitimately produces nothing while it waits for a keyframe,
and produces nothing for ever if what arrives is not the codec it was opened for, which
is a fault anywhere in the sender or the network. The list could not take effect either,
since discovery runs once per process behind dyn_done[], nor persist, since it lived in
process memory. What remains is selection-time fallback: candidates are ranked and probed,
and a MIME with no creatable decoder registers no codec, which leaves libvpx to answer
for VP8.

Finally, it carries the ENCODER output-buffer ledger, which is instrumentation and not a
behaviour change. Correcting the descriptor above turned a black remote tile into moving
video and exposed the next defect behind it: 1001 sends for a while and then stops, and
the only thing the log says is "Encoder failed to get input Buffer", 1250 to 1721 times,
where a pre-fix run said it zero times. That message is the least informative one this
file can produce, because an exhausted OUTPUT pool is exactly what it looks like from the
input side -- MediaCodec reports a full output pool by ceasing to offer input buffers, two
functions and several seconds away from the path that lost the buffer.

So the invariant is measured rather than argued about. Every index taken out of
enc_avail_output_buf must reach exactly one AMediaCodec_releaseOutputBuffer, on every
control-flow path, and three counters separate the three fates an offered buffer can have:

    offered   (out-cb)      onOutputAvailable fired
    acquired  (acq)         dequeued by encode_begin; ours to release
    discarded (discarded)   overwritten inside the queue; unreleasable

offered - acquired - discarded is what is still queued, which is normal and bounded.
acquired - released is what this codec holds right now, which must never exceed one.
discarded is a leak with no other symptom at all, and it is real: pj_atomic_queue's put()
advances the READ pointer when the next write would land on it, so the sixteenth item
silently destroys the first, returns PJ_SUCCESS, and takes a buffer index with it that
nobody can now release.

A static audit of this file finds no path from the acquisition to a return that skips the
release -- which is itself the finding, because the hold spans a RETURN to pjmedia whenever
a frame needs more than one RTP packet. encode_begin hands the buffer to encode_more, and
it is vid_stream's put_frame loop that calls encode_more again. put_frame has two returns
inside that loop ("RTP encode_rtp() error" and "Cannot allocate send entry") which leave it
while has_more is still TRUE, and nothing in this file then executes at all. enc_frame_open
catches that: a frame still open when the next one is acquired had its buffer abandoned by
the caller, and the buffer is released there rather than lost, with a line naming how many
fragments and bytes of which frame were in flight.

The same accounting is kept for the decoder's output pool, which drains the same way and
reports it the same way, and both halves are printed at teardown -- where the exit
condition for this work is read off: acquired == released, discarded == 0, on both halves.

The +3-byte question the descriptor fix raises is answered in the same place and by
arithmetic rather than assumption. pjmedia_vpx_packetize caps a fragment at mtu - desc and
only then refuses if fragment + desc exceeds the caller's out_size, so the descriptor can
matter only when out_size < mtu. out_size here is the stream's whole-frame buffer and mtu
is enc_mtu; the encoder logs both once per stream with the margin between them, and
pktz=ok/err counts every call either way.

Finally, it closes the defect the ledger above was built to find, and the ledger itself
stays in because it is what makes the invariant checkable.

Correcting the descriptor turned a black remote tile into moving video and exposed the
next failure behind it: 1001 sent for a while and then stopped, with nothing in the log
but "Encoder failed to get input Buffer", 1250 to 1721 times where a pre-fix run said it
zero times. That message is the least informative one this file can produce, because an
exhausted OUTPUT pool is exactly what it looks like from the input side.

The ledger separated the three fates an offered buffer can have -- offered (out-cb),
acquired (acq), discarded -- and the answer was immediate and was not a leak:

    15:26:49  in-cb=849  out-cb=845  acq=845  rel=845   diff 0
    15:27:00  in-cb=873  out-cb=873  acq=869  rel=869   diff 4   <- both callbacks stop
    15:31:20  in-cb=873  out-cb=873  acq=869  rel=869   camera still at 30fps

acq == rel throughout, held-max == 1, discarded == 0, abandoned == 0: every buffer this
codec ACQUIRED reached exactly one release on every path. What it never did was acquire
the last four. encode_begin dequeued the INPUT index first and, when that queue was empty,
returned without ever touching the output queue -- so each starved call left one index
MediaCodec had already offered sitting in enc_avail_output_buf, never acquired, never
released, never returned to a pool of four. A component with no free output buffer cannot
encode, and a component that cannot encode stops offering INPUT buffers. The starvation
was self-reinforcing, and the message reporting it named the wrong half of the codec.

Measured on two handsets with two different components -- c2.exynos.vp8.encoder on an
SM-M146B and c2.android.vp8.encoder on an SM-E236B -- both froze at exactly four stranded
(2026-09-25). It also retro-explains the note in 0002 about each replacement encoder
lasting a shorter time than the last: the deadlock drained every new component's pool the
same way, faster each time because starvation was already dense when the swap began.
Healthy encoders were being condemned for this.

The fix is to stop coupling the two halves. Feeding an input frame and collecting an
encoded one are independent: an encoded frame waiting for us came from an EARLIER input
and has nothing to do with the one we just missed. So the input attempt is allowed to
fail, and the output half runs either way. The same coupling existed in and_media_decode
and is fixed the same way, with a drain_output label rather than three bare returns; the
mid-picture !write_output return is deliberately left alone, because nothing has been
queued at that point and it cannot strand anything.

The same class of defect, one step earlier: an input index that was dequeued but never
filled -- getInputBuffer returning NULL, the buffer being too small, queueInputBuffer
failing -- was dropped rather than handed back. The decoder ledger named it exactly,
in-cb - queued == null == 3 against a pool of four. Those four paths now return the index
to the component with a zero-length queueInputBuffer, which is what the recovery at the
top of and_media_codec_decode already did for its own case.

Measured end to end, same call, before and after:

    encoder in-empty (the symptom)   9534 of 10407 encode_begin  ->  0 of 11043
    encoder frames                   869                         ->  11041
    zero-size frames out             9538                        ->  2
    out-cb - acq                     climbs to 4, then frozen    ->  2, bounded
    acq vs rel at teardown           869 / 869                   ->  11041 / 11041
    discarded / abandoned            0 / 0                       ->  0 / 0
    sustained encode rate            4.6 -> 3.1 fps, then zero   ->  30 fps for 5.7 min

There is no restart, no condemnation, no timeout, no mid-call codec swap and no change to
bitrate, resolution or frame rate anywhere in this patch. The deadlock was a missing
drain; the fix is the drain.

It also validates the VP8 frame at the DECODER's input boundary, which is instrumentation
rather than a behaviour change. c2.android.vp8.decoder on an SM-E236B reports
"work failed to complete: 14" (C2_CORRUPTED) partway into every call and then produces no
further pictures, with this codec's buffer ledger perfectly balanced and nothing held --
so the question is whether the bytes handed to it were already malformed. That cannot be
answered by blaming either side, and RFC 6386 s9.1 makes it checkable without a decoder:
the 3-byte frame tag carries the frame type, version, show_frame and the first partition
size, and a key frame then carries the start code 9d 01 2a. Three properties are verified
before every queueInputBuffer -- the start code on key frames, the first partition fitting
inside the frame, and the frame being long enough for its own header -- and counted as
ok/bad.

Counted alongside them is something this file already did and had no name for: the number
of ZERO-LENGTH buffers handed to the decoder. A zero-length buffer is not a valid VP8
frame, and there are two places that produce one -- the recovery at the top of
and_media_codec_decode returning an index the previous decode acquired but never queued,
and the null-buffer handback above. The first fires on every picture with a packet hole,
because vid_stream passes missing packets through as zero-size entries and decode_vpx
abandons the picture at the first one. If the component's error count tracks
dec_empty_queued rather than dec_vp8_bad, the recovery is what is feeding it rubbish; the
teardown line prints all three so the two can be told apart rather than argued about.

Finally, it stops submitting partial VP8 pictures to the decoder, which is what the
validator above was added to detect and then found.

At ~12% packet loss the receive path was handing MediaCodec frames that were not frames:
a 5232-byte picture declaring VP8 version 4 (only 0..3 exist) and a 477516-byte first
partition, and on an SM-E236B the single malformed picture of a call was followed 4 ms
later by "c2.android.vp8.decoder: work failed to complete: 14" (C2_CORRUPTED), after which
that component produced nothing for the rest of the call. The sender was not at fault --
pktz reported 17176 packetizations and 0 errors, and the keyframe start-code guard never
fired -- and neither was the decoder: it was handed rubbish.

vid_stream assembles a picture from whatever the jitter buffer holds and passes packets it
never received through as zero-size entries, so a loss anywhere in a frame used to be
submitted anyway. Four distinct ways a picture could reach the decoder incomplete were
found and each is now a whole-picture drop taken BEFORE any byte enters an input buffer:

  * the frame beginning is missing. Required is `S == 1 && PID == 0` in the payload
    descriptor's first byte (|X|R|N|S|R| PID |). S alone is NOT sufficient: it marks the
    start of a VP8 PARTITION, and S with a non-zero PID begins partition 1..7, which is
    mid-frame. pjmedia_vpx_packetize sets S only at bits_pos 0 and never writes PID, so a
    frame we sent starts with S=1 and PID=0 exactly once.

  * a hole anywhere in the middle -- any zero-size entry in the packet array.

  * a truncated tail. The 3-byte frame tag declares the first partition's size, so a whole
    frame is at least hdr + part1 bytes; a keyframe whose later packets were lost arrives
    as a single 1300-byte payload declaring a 1469-byte first partition. The RTP marker bit
    would say the same thing but vid_stream zeroes bit_info, so the bitstream's own
    declaration is used instead and nothing new is plumbed through the layers above.

  * a packet whose bytes could not be taken. When and_media_get_input_buffer fails
    mid-picture, and_media_decode returns PJ_SUCCESS so the drain still runs -- and
    decode_vpx used to carry on to the next packet, which then wrote from offset 0 and
    restarted the frame at a mid-frame byte. Measured: 5 packets, valid head, 6266 bytes
    assembled, 4966 queued, the missing 1300 being packet 0. dec_pic_broken now abandons
    the picture instead.

A dropped picture produces no output, and the caller already publishes
PJMEDIA_EVENT_KEYFRAME_MISSING in that case, so the stream asks for the keyframe that
resumes it without any new machinery. Nothing is fabricated, no header is prepended, no
component is restarted, and no decoder-specific special case is added.

Verified on the same lossy network, both handsets, after the change:

    invalid frames reaching decoder   bad=0        (was 1-2 per call)
    C2_CORRUPTED                      0            (was 1 per call on the M23)
    dropped pictures                  >0           (expected, and counted)
    MediaCodec ledgers                acq == rel, held=0, discarded=0

max-input-size is deliberately left at 0. A real bound was tried on the theory that a
picture was being split across input buffers; it was not -- that picture had lost a packet
to a failed acquisition -- and the component ignores the hint regardless, reporting a
7340032-byte buffer whether asked for 128 KB, 0.5 MB or 1.37 MB.

Finally, it stops a hardware encoder and a hardware decoder of the same format from
contending for one video engine, and repairs two things that only the software decoder
was strict enough to expose.

A hardware VP8 encoder and a hardware VP8 decoder can be two clients of a single engine
inside one vendor Codec2 service, and it serialises them. Measured on an SM-M146B with
c2.exynos.vp8.encoder plus c2.exynos.vp8.decoder, both hardware, both hosted by
samsung.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service (2026-09-26):

    encode alone                   30.0 fps
    decode alone                   26.6 fps
    both together    encode 4.4 fps, decode 0.5 fps

The encoder returned to 30.0 fps within three seconds of the decoder going idle, in the
same call, with four and a half CPU cores unused. The platform expressed the limit as
availability, not as an error: both components were offered input buffers at an identical
4.4/s while this codec's ownership ledger stayed balanced, so it was the component
withholding work rather than a buffer we failed to return. Thermal, battery level, CPU
frequency and thread saturation were each measured and excluded.

So when the chosen encoder for a MIME is a hardware component, the decoder now takes the
best NON-hardware candidate first, falling back to hardware if none can be created. The
expensive half keeps the engine; the cheap half runs on the software component every
device also advertises. The far handset in that same call ran software VP8 in both
directions on a weaker SoC at 30 fps, which is the measurement that makes this a trade
rather than a guess. The only input is isHardwareAccelerated() as the platform reports
it -- no device, SoC, Android version or component name appears anywhere, nothing is
swapped mid-call, and libvpx remains the fallback it always was.

Two defects surfaced once a software decoder was actually in the path:

  * an index whose getInputBuffer() returned NULL was handed back with a zero-length
    queueInputBuffer. A zero-length buffer is not a valid VP8 frame:
    c2.android.vp8.decoder went ALLOCATED -> RELEASING -> RELEASED with a C2 error before
    decoding a single picture. A NULL buffer means the index was never validly ours, so it
    is now dropped rather than returned.

  * the decoder was configured with dec_fmt's geometry, which for VP8 is pjmedia's max-fs
    placeholder -- an AREA in macroblocks rendered as the largest SQUARE, 1088x1088. A
    1280x720 stream is legal under that area yet 192 pixels wider than the square. The
    hardware component tolerated being configured narrower than its input; the software one
    answered three of its first four input callbacks with NULL buffers and never started.
    The configured geometry is now widened to MAX_RX_WIDTH/HEIGHT, the same bound
    dec_buf_size is sized from, so one number states the largest picture this file accepts.

Measured after these changes, same handsets, same call, both directions:

    1001  capture 30.0  assembled 29.8  encoded 25.5  decoded 29.7   INVALID 0  C2 0
    1005  capture 29.9  assembled 25.4  encoded 29.9  decoded 25.2   INVALID 0  C2 0
    decoder ledger  in-cb 224  in-ok 224  in-empty 1  null 0
                    out-cb 224  acq 220  rel 220  held 0  discarded 0

Two further corrections came out of running a software decoder in anger.

A freshly created VP8 decoder holds no reference frame, so the first picture fed to it must
be a keyframe. This file did not check, and on a decoder recreated mid-stream -- by a video
off/on, or by a call that happened to join mid-GOP -- the first input was an inter-frame
referencing pictures the new component had never seen. c2.android.vp8.decoder does not
merely fail to decode that; it stops. Captured at the boundary, healthy against failed:

    healthy   CB_IN 0,1,2,3
              DEQ+GET idx=0 -> buf=0x717fe28000   QUEUE idx=0 size=1574
              CB_IN idx=0 (cb#5)                  <- component returns the index
              DEQ+GET idx=1 -> buf=0x717fc28000   QUEUE idx=1 size=41  ...

    failed    CB_IN 0,1,2,3
              DEQ+GET idx=0 -> buf=0x717fc2c000   QUEUE idx=0 size=7126
              DEQ+GET idx=1 -> buf=0x0
              DEQ+GET idx=2 -> buf=0x0
              DEQ+GET idx=3 -> buf=0x0            <- and never another callback

The NULLs were the aftermath, not the fault: the component died on the first queued frame
and then returned nothing for any index. So the reassembly now discards pictures until the
first keyframe after a decoder start, counted as vp8_frames_drop_no_keyframe. The caller
already publishes PJMEDIA_EVENT_KEYFRAME_MISSING when a decode yields nothing, which is what
asks the far end for the keyframe that starts the stream -- no new machinery, no restart, no
retry.

The second is the selection policy itself. A format that is encoded and decoded at the same
time now prefers software components for BOTH halves, under
AND_MEDIA_BIDIR_PREFER_SOFTWARE. Three configurations were measured on the same handset:

    hardware encoder + hardware decoder   encode 4.4 fps, decode 0.5 fps; the encoder
                                          recovered to 30 fps within three seconds of the
                                          decoder going idle -- one engine, serialised
    hardware encoder + software decoder   nondeterministic across identical runs
    software encoder + software decoder   five consecutive clean startups, a ten-minute
                                          soak, and a full lifecycle matrix

Concurrency cannot be validated at selection time without a configure-and-start probe, and
this file refuses those for good reason. So the safe pair is chosen deterministically. It is
a policy and not a device rule: the only input is the platform's own isHardwareAccelerated(),
no model, SoC, Android version or component name is consulted, nothing is swapped mid-call,
and setting the macro to 0 restores hardware-first.

Measured on the final build, 1001 <-> 1005, ten minutes:

    1001  capture 30.0  assembled 29.8  encoded 22.7  decoded 29.3   INVALID 0  C2 0
    1005  capture 30.0  assembled 22.6  encoded 30.0  decoded 21.8   INVALID 0  C2 0
    encoder  acq 17589  rel 17589  held 0  discarded 0  abandoned 0  pktz 93602/0
    decoder  acq 23106  rel 23106  held 0  discarded 0  null 0
